# Create kibana_sdp_amdb_enrich: the single, keyword-mapped source of truth that
# replaces the text+.keyword kibana_sdp_amdb.
#
# Why this index exists
# ---------------------
# kibana_sdp_amdb maps hostname/group/alert_status as `text` with a `.keyword`
# multi-field. That shape blocks the ENRICH route (enrich cannot use a
# multi-field subfield as match_field) and forces awkward `.keyword` naming in
# the LOOKUP JOIN route. You cannot change a field's type in place, so the fix is
# a new index with those fields as plain `keyword`. Rather than keep it as a
# reindex mirror of kibana_sdp_amdb, this makes it the source of truth: you write
# records here directly and retire kibana_sdp_amdb.
#
# One index, both routes
# ----------------------
# Created with keyword mappings AND index.mode: lookup, so it serves either rule:
#   - LOOKUP JOIN (10008_single_stage_enrich_index): references it directly; needs
#     it ON prod, populated; reads live (no policy, no execute).
#   - ENRICH     (10008_single_stage_enrich_variant): kibana_sdp_amdb_enrich_policy
#     builds an enrich policy on it, LOCAL only; needs _execute after every change.
# index.mode: lookup only constrains number_of_shards to 1 (fine for a small
# config index) and does NOT restrict writes -- verified in IndexMode.java 9.3 --
# so you can add and delete records directly. Enrich has no source-index-mode
# restriction, so a lookup-mode index is a valid enrich source too.
#
# WHERE to create it
#   - Going with LOOKUP JOIN  -> create on prod (that is where the join resolves
#     it). You may also keep a local copy if anything local queries it.
#   - Going with ENRICH       -> create on the local/CCS cluster (the coordinator
#     runs the enrich there).

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Create the index
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# group is multi-valued per host (a list); keyword holds arrays natively, and the
# rules MV_EXPAND it. Only the fields the rules read are typed here; add any other
# AMDB fields you want to carry into tickets later (all as keyword or the right
# scalar type -- avoid re-introducing text+.keyword on anything you match/join on).
PUT kibana_sdp_amdb_enrich
{
  "settings": {
    "index.mode": "lookup"
  },
  "mappings": {
    "properties": {
      "hostname":     { "type": "keyword" },
      "group":        { "type": "keyword" },
      "alert_status": { "type": "keyword" }
    }
  }
}

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. One-time migration from kibana_sdp_amdb
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Copies existing config across once. After this, kibana_sdp_amdb_enrich is the
# source of truth -- do NOT keep reindexing; point your write path here instead
# (see below). The text -> keyword change happens because the destination mapping
# says keyword; the stored _source values are unchanged. group arrives as an
# array and stays one.
#
# If migrating to a copy on a DIFFERENT cluster (e.g. local kibana_sdp_amdb ->
# prod kibana_sdp_amdb_enrich), use reindex-from-remote instead of this local
# reindex -- see kibana_sdp_amdb_prod_lookup_index for the remote form.
POST _reindex
{
  "source": {
    "index": "kibana_sdp_amdb",
    "_source": ["hostname", "group", "alert_status"]
  },
  "dest": { "index": "kibana_sdp_amdb_enrich" }
}

# Verify the migration: count should match kibana_sdp_amdb, hostnames should look
# like real hostnames, and group should be an array where a host has several.
GET kibana_sdp_amdb_enrich/_count
GET kibana_sdp_amdb_enrich/_search
{ "size": 5, "_source": ["hostname", "group", "alert_status"] }

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Make it the source of truth
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# From here on, all config edits go DIRECTLY to kibana_sdp_amdb_enrich:
#
#   # add / update a host (upsert by a stable _id, e.g. the hostname)
#   PUT kibana_sdp_amdb_enrich/_doc/CS01-10VLCNPY24
#   {
#     "hostname": "CS01-10VLCNPY24",
#     "group": ["10008", "10004"],          # ARRAY -- one entry per alert enabled
#     "alert_status": "enabled"
#   }
#
#   # remove a host
#   DELETE kibana_sdp_amdb_enrich/_doc/CS01-10VLCNPY24
#
# Repoint whatever process currently populates kibana_sdp_amdb at this index. Two
# things to get right in that write path:
#   - group must be written as a JSON ARRAY, not a comma-joined string, or the
#     MV_EXPAND / "group == 10008" filter will not behave.
#   - use a stable document _id (the hostname is the natural choice) so updates
#     upsert instead of creating duplicates.
#
# LOOKUP JOIN sees these writes LIVE. ENRICH does not -- you must re-execute the
# policy after each change (see kibana_sdp_amdb_enrich_policy); that is inherent
# to enrich, not something this index changes.

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Retiring kibana_sdp_amdb -- SEQUENCE CAREFULLY
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Do NOT delete kibana_sdp_amdb yet. The live two-rule system (10008 Stage 2 and
# every other *_Stage 2 rule) still does `LOOKUP JOIN kibana_sdp_amdb ON
# hostname.keyword`. Deleting it breaks production.
#
# Safe order:
#   1. create + migrate kibana_sdp_amdb_enrich (steps 1-2)
#   2. move the write path to it (step 3), keeping kibana_sdp_amdb updated in
#      parallel for now, OR accept that only new rules read the new index
#   3. cut each consolidated rule over (Test query -> enable -> disable the old
#      Stage 1/Stage 2 pair) -- see 10008_single_stage_README.md cutover
#   4. only once NOTHING references kibana_sdp_amdb:
#        DELETE kibana_sdp_amdb
#
# Naming note: "_enrich" is a legacy of how this started; it is really the general
# AMDB source of truth now and is used by the LOOKUP JOIN route too. Rename if you
# like -- if you do, update the index name in 10008_single_stage_enrich_index and
# the `indices` in kibana_sdp_amdb_enrich_policy to match.
